#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119643 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "01f32313-178c-5626-91af-110907202416" :question/math-academy-id "e-4936" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(9,-2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}=3\\mathbf{i}-4\\mathbf{j}+7\\mathbf{k}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 9,-2,3 \\rangle$ and the normal vector of the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 3,-4,7 \\rangle.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =⟨9,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =56 \\\\ 3x-4y+7z & =56 \\\\ 3x-4y+7z-56 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119644 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/id #uuid "d92eb242-104f-55cd-82dc-7620e2f4e636" :question/math-academy-id "e-4937" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-1,4,8)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\dfrac{x+3}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}{3}=\\dfrac{z+2}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -1,4,8 \\rangle.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =⟨-1,4,8⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z-33 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119645 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119834 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "aa7c3400-9bc0-5f3a-84e5-f3cc2bb87f5d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4938" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $2x-y+z+13=0$ of the plane $\\Pi,$ find an equation of $\\Pi$ in the form $(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot\\mathbf{n}=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the coefficients in equation of the plane, we get that the normal vector of $\\Pi$ is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2,-1,1 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\Pi$ for $z\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z+13 & =0 \\\\ z & =-2x+y-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0,$ we get $z=-13.$ So, the point $P(0,0,-13)$ lies on $\\Pi.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 0,0,-13 \\rangle.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-13⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,-1,1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119646 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119835 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "8a9e1070-0ca0-5f24-ac89-999c9271a91b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4939" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,2,-6)$ and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{v_1}=\\langle -3, 5, 2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}=\\langle 1, 3, -2 \\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,-6 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-16i-4j-14k \\\\  & =⟨-16,-4,-14⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =⟨-3,2,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =124\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =124 \\\\ -16x-4y-14z & =124 \\\\ -16x-4y-14z-124 & =0 \\\\ 8x+2y+7z+62 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d071325-4ae4-5964-a64f-fab8d162533c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-3y+2z-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260960} {:answer/id #uuid "be614cfc-c9fb-5c76-8dee-684a30dc17e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-3y+z-17=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261553} {:answer/id #uuid "ce192468-5857-54ba-b341-740fd51b914a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-y+2z-11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261695} {:answer/id #uuid "e488cd1b-dabd-58e2-8323-9fd47b3dbc39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y+3z-15=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261885} {:answer/id #uuid "ed08e876-cf68-52de-82e6-d95fc85813d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-2y+z-13=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce192468-5857-54ba-b341-740fd51b914a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-y+2z-11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261695} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ed27435-6a6b-5a91-82c1-435b9c8e60ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1247c7f3-ac99-5899-948f-91e6b23f17f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-127964" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(5,2,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=3i-j+2k.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨5,2,-1⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨3,-1,2⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-1,2⟩ & =⟨5,2,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-1,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-1,2⟩ & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-1,2⟩ & =11 \\\\ 3x-y+2z & =11 \\\\ 3x-y+2z-11 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119835 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f5bbffd-b662-53da-9146-542052fb7f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x+5y+5z+1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413719} {:answer/id #uuid "88552711-7258-529e-8575-2c6ac297ae0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5x+5y+5z+10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413721} {:answer/id #uuid "88a1e7b7-4a66-5740-9c7e-14a39dd3849b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x+5y+5z-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413722} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f975fc-9692-533a-ba00-bf7e8438a517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-5y+5z-10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413726} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ddaec5-2f45-5d0e-8c08-e6549f59e357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5x+5y+5z+5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88552711-7258-529e-8575-2c6ac297ae0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5x+5y+5z+10=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413721} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3cdc898-7c65-52bc-9785-8f3da0b98b76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fc9c30e8-905e-5cc3-9596-440fe740820a" :question/math-academy-id "q-128410" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(1,-2,1)$, $P(3,-1,2)$ and $Q(4,2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨1,-2,1⟩,\\,p=⟨3,-1,2⟩,$ and $q=⟨4,2,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,2⟩-⟨1,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨4,2,0⟩-⟨1,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,4,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-5i+5j+5k \\\\  & =⟨-5,5,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,5,5⟩ & =⟨3,-1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,5,5⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,5,5⟩ & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,5,5⟩ & =-10 \\\\ -5x+5y+5z & =-10 \\\\ -5x+5y+5z+10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302310 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119834 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50e8d53b-177d-5796-8cf7-52c015fc02a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50e8d53b-177d-5796-8cf7-52c015fc02a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300384} :answer-field/id #uuid "11b7d2ed-536b-5849-8e7d-54f3dbfbb735" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301284} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8de30fa2-0c67-5646-bab5-d1acac95ffaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8de30fa2-0c67-5646-bab5-d1acac95ffaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300681} :answer-field/id #uuid "2eae7c05-5e89-5f2f-b6dd-83424a9cf5ce" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301372} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27084236-e409-5976-ba9d-d171f96debd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300148}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27084236-e409-5976-ba9d-d171f96debd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300148} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d7b439f-d927-5a9b-940c-4e51f8e79127" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8f8613f-ede8-5b23-9e9a-6a90b747b545" :question/math-academy-id "q-326901" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $2x-y+2z+6=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$\n\n$(r-⟨0,0,$ \n{{field-1}}\n$⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,$\n{{field-2}}\n, \n{{field-3}}\n$⟩=0$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨2,-1,2⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y+2z+6 & =0 \\\\ z & =-x+\\frac{y}{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-3.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-3)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,-3⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-3⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,-1,2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34272b7-a7ad-5a58-974e-54cca957d06e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2717e35a-d5d8-5b8d-b82d-51c22326e847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x-y+z+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413718} {:answer/id #uuid "9f69fd47-a1f5-5770-8c3d-1d9916854264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+z-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413723} {:answer/id #uuid "a34b8674-3166-5800-b2b6-3d8e2d749e97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+z+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413725} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1a451c-30a6-598a-a1e4-37120fd4f9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4y+z+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413728} {:answer/id #uuid "cf32c54e-7d9c-57e0-ac44-14a3df665bca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+y+z+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a34b8674-3166-5800-b2b6-3d8e2d749e97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+z+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413725} :answer-field/id #uuid "261a61c5-5d9c-5e73-a9c8-99e605e6c3e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d60eaae3-858a-5d2f-83b8-ccadfd1429d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-330499" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-1,3,2)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨4,0,1⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-1,3,2⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨4,0,1⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,0,1⟩ & =⟨-1,3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨4,0,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,0,1⟩ & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨4,0,1⟩ & =-2 \\\\ 4x+z & =-2 \\\\ 4x+z+2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120283 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119835 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0efe5b11-2c87-58e9-bf7c-e1eecb07adf2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Parallel To Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bf34d07-cd99-5983-8a1b-cb31892f721e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-2y+z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312064} {:answer/id #uuid "543cf762-98f0-583c-b80f-a17527258a20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5y+5z-16=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312656} {:answer/id #uuid "81d82da8-094c-5d53-92cb-5fda4924d683" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5y+5z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313021} {:answer/id #uuid "869b5aba-db3e-5905-b9d5-c7f7146ea7fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x-2y+z+16=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313053} {:answer/id #uuid "d14dfaa9-4c8e-55a3-b867-d30669e90de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+5y+5z+16=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186313609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "543cf762-98f0-583c-b80f-a17527258a20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5y+5z-16=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312656} :answer-field/id #uuid "9200a78c-c2dd-5fc5-8cd7-e5930b0a22e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93f0f0ea-5609-5473-8b26-f85225442381" :question/math-academy-id "q-33912" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,0,3)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨5,2,1⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,3,3⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨1,0,3⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =3i-15j+15k \\\\  & =⟨3,-15,15⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ & =⟨1,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ & =48 \\\\ 3x-15y+15z & =48 \\\\ 3x-15y+15z-48 & =0 \\\\ x-5y+5z-16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338826 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e82b227-9a56-5114-8d60-f1db519ee0d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x+y+z-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336168} {:answer/id #uuid "8f404ec9-8911-54fa-8613-a6198b2c338f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y-z-14=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337324} {:answer/id #uuid "a94ce30c-4842-5698-bc28-64b32c7460e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y-2z-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337560} {:answer/id #uuid "b98b2fc3-d714-56e3-8598-185596415529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-z-14=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337676} {:answer/id #uuid "bad68854-37a9-54dc-a14b-ae58354b47a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y-2z-14=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bad68854-37a9-54dc-a14b-ae58354b47a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y-2z-14=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337689} :answer-field/id #uuid "23126fc2-1b82-57e0-8166-f43f5a36d945" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "743ee1e7-8110-5025-8c98-4fb7a2685168" :question/math-academy-id "q-70809" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,3,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨2,3,-1⟩.$ From the vectorial equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨3,2,-2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨3,2,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,2,-2⟩ & =⟨2,3,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨3,2,-2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,2,-2⟩ & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,2,-2⟩ & =14 \\\\ 3x+2y-2z & =14 \\\\ 3x+2y-2z-14 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2173739c-cd59-58ed-a265-8ee88c982ae0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of the Plane Perpendicular To a Given Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2493b28e-9805-515c-9bb7-52400b1ebad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+5y-z+3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413717} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7649c7-0f92-5513-b462-907271b682a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3z+3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413720} {:answer/id #uuid "a11ad414-7674-5c26-950f-59f6a219f5d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-z+3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413724} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c26dd6-942b-51e3-9221-ce5b6eff63d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+5y-z-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413730} {:answer/id #uuid "d4795ff7-792e-54da-b148-8c4ee1d289d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+5y+z-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2493b28e-9805-515c-9bb7-52400b1ebad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+5y-z+3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413717} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6c20a87-b8c2-530d-b3e1-fb8bd507d26c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34ca6320-0524-59fc-8f68-7b8524566ac6" :question/math-academy-id "q-70815" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(0,0,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=2+2t \\\\ y=5t \\\\ z=1-t\\end{aligned}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,3⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨2,5,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨2,5,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,5,-1⟩ & =⟨0,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,5,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,5,-1⟩ & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,5,-1⟩ & =-3 \\\\ 2x+5y-z & =-3 \\\\ 2x+5y-z+3 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119834 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80c443e5-a0b2-5aae-ac8d-27449a8049a3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Plane Given Its Cartesian Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d75490e5-1509-58bb-9763-c438183b853c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,2⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,0,-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393017} {:answer/id #uuid "aeda2ffa-42b4-52c6-88ee-ca624f7cbd32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,-2⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,0,-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393018} {:answer/id #uuid "e668ab95-81b2-5487-a8db-a7ad8d3973d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,2⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,1,-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393019} {:answer/id #uuid "9538cda3-e06b-5fe5-b129-c566cce61ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,-2⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,1,-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393020} {:answer/id #uuid "937f4290-2d11-5090-a4a2-afbc778336d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,-2⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,-2,-4⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9538cda3-e06b-5fe5-b129-c566cce61ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,-2⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,1,-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393020} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca2ac9ba-0e12-54e9-981f-caf50c21ada3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73c1d5d9-6a23-5438-8d9f-6779511254e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-70818" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $2x+y-2z-4=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨2,1,-2⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y-2z-4 & =0 \\\\ -2z & =-2x-y+4 \\\\ z & =x+\\frac{1}{2}y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-2.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-2)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,-2⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-2⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,1,-2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
